CONTEXT FROM TEXTBOOK:
This figure shows a schematic diagram of the Geiger-Rutherford gold foil experiment, depicting the complete apparatus from alpha-particle source to detection. On the left, a labeled radium source of alpha particles emits a collimated green beam that travels horizontally to the right toward a thin gold foil mounted at the center of a circular luminescent screen, with the screen encircling the foil to capture scattered particles at any angle. Four labeled outcomes illustrate the three types of trajectories observed: most alpha particles pass straight through the foil undeflected, a few are slightly deflected at small angles, and a very small number are significantly deflected at large angles, including backward toward the source. The circular luminescent screen enables detection across the full angular range, making even the rare large-angle backscattering events observable. This figure introduces the experimental setup whose unexpected results — particularly the small fraction of particles deflected at large angles — provided the empirical evidence that Rutherford used to reject the Thomson plum-pudding model and propose instead a nuclear model of the atom with charge and mass concentrated in a tiny central nucleus.

PROJECTION TYPE: axonometric
Use ordinary orthographic projection: setCameraView({ projection: 'orthographic', axisScreenAngles, heightFraction }). If the plan gives axis_screen_angles: null (a flat 2D plot/diagram), pass axisScreenAngles: null for a front-on view. Otherwise the plan does not measure per-figure angles — use a standard isometric pose: axisScreenAngles: { x: 30, z: 30 }. The source image is a PROJECTION of a 3D object: rebuild the true object — a rectangle stays a rectangle in world space — and let the camera do the projecting. Never bake the drawn parallelogram into the geometry, and never shear geometry. Omit zoom and call setCameraView after all geometry is added — the scaffold auto-fits from the projected bounding box.

ELEMENTS TO RECREATE IN 3D:
  - Rectangular lead source housing (box) at far left, drawn with a shaded gray gradient and a visible top face and right-hand end face
  - Dashed rectangular outline inside the housing marking the hollow bore/cavity that collimates the beam
  - Small bright green radium source dot with white halo at the left end of the internal cavity
  - Small dark rectangular aperture (collimator slit) on the right-hand face of the housing where the beam exits
  - Straight horizontal green alpha-particle beam running from the source dot, out of the aperture, across open space to the gold foil
  - Thin upright gold foil plate (yellow/orange) standing at the exact center of the circular screen
  - Circular luminescent screen: a vertical-axis cylindrical band (ring/hoop) with gray outer surface and pale green inner surface, encircling the foil, open at the top
  - Bundle of undeflected trajectory lines continuing straight through the foil to the far side of the ring
  - Small-angle deflected trajectory lines fanning a few degrees off-axis forward of the foil
  - Large-angle deflected trajectory lines, including several going backward/sideways toward the source side of the ring
  - Bright scintillation flash spots where each trajectory terminates on the inner surface of the screen
  - Leader lines with text labels: 'Radium source of α particles', 'Beam of α particles', 'Luminescent screen to detect scattered α particles', 'Thin gold foil', 'Most α particles pass straight through foil', 'A few α particles are slightly deflected', 'A very small number of α particles are significantly deflected'

GEOMETRY NOTES (world-space scale, color palette, and any formula/value needed for correctness — build to these so sizes, zoom, and colors match the source; do not guess dimensions the notes already specify):
World axes: +X screen-right (beam direction), +Y screen-up, +Z toward viewer. Whole scene spans about x ∈ [-9, +7], y ∈ [-3, +3]. SOURCE HOUSING: rectangular box 3.4 (X) × 2.0 (Y) × 2.2 (Z), centered at (-7.0, 0, 0), sitting so the beam axis y=0 passes through its middle; shaded gray gradient from dark #4C5754 on the front face to light #D3DAD7 on the top face, black outline. Inside it, a dashed thin-line rectangle 2.4 × 0.8 × 0.8 marks the hollow bore, centered at (-7.0, 0, 0). Radium source: sphere r=0.16, emissive green #76C043 with a white ring, at (-8.0, 0, 0), the left end of the bore. Collimator aperture: dark rectangle 0.35 × 0.7 on the +X face at (-5.3, 0, 0). BEAM: cylinder radius 0.09, green #76C043, from (-8.0,0,0) to the foil at (+3.6,0,0); it is a single straight horizontal line — do not curve or fan it before the foil. SCREEN: open-top cylindrical band, axis vertical (+Y), inner radius 2.5, wall thickness 0.12, height 1.25, centered at (+3.6, 0, 0) so the foil sits at its exact center; outer surface gray #A8AEAB with a darker top rim #8C918E, inner surface pale mint #DCEBDC. The beam enters through the −X side of the ring and the undeflected bundle exits through the +X side. GOLD FOIL: thin upright plate 0.12 (X) × 1.0 (Y) × 0.55 (Z) at (+3.6, 0, 0), standing vertically and roughly face-on to the beam (tilted ~10° about Y so its face catches light), fill #F5C13A with a darker #D89B1E edge. TRAJECTORIES: thin green #8DCE4A lines, all originating at the foil point (+3.6,0,0) and terminating on the inner ring wall at radius 2.5. Straight-through bundle: 3–5 lines within ±3° of +X. Slight deflections: ~6 lines at 8°–25° off +X, spread both in the horizontal plane and slightly up/down. Large deflections: ~4 lines at 60°–170° off the beam axis, at least two going back past 90° toward the source side. Each line ends in a small bright scintillation disc (r≈0.08, #CFFF9E) on the inner wall. Counts by thickness slider t (layers, 1–20): straight-through fraction ≈ 1, slight-deflection line count ≈ round(2 + 0.8·t) capped at ~16, large-angle line count fixed at 2–4 regardless of t. LABELS: black sans-serif text with thin black leader lines — 'Radium source of α particles' below-left pointing up to the source dot; 'Beam of α particles' above the midpoint of the beam pointing down; 'Luminescent screen to detect scattered α particles' below-center pointing up to the near-front of the ring; 'Thin gold foil' below-right pointing up to the foil; 'Most α particles pass straight through foil' top-right pointing down to the forward bundle; 'A few α particles are slightly deflected' top-center-right pointing down to the small-angle fan; 'A very small number of α particles are significantly deflected' top-center-left pointing down-right to a back-scattered line.

DISCRETE CONTROLS (implement every one of these in #ui, each must work independently):
[
  {
    "id": "fireBeam",
    "type": "animation",
    "label": "Fire the α-particle beam",
    "teaches": "The overwhelming majority of alpha particles pass straight through the foil; only occasionally does one veer off, and very rarely one bounces back — so the scattering centers must be tiny and sparse.",
    "loop": true
  },
  {
    "id": "foilThickness",
    "type": "slider",
    "label": "Gold foil thickness (atomic layers)",
    "teaches": "Adding atomic layers multiplies the number of small-angle deflections (more chances to graze an atom) while the rate of rare large-angle backscatter stays essentially unchanged — the hard scattering centers occupy a vanishingly small fraction of the foil's area.",
    "range": [
      1,
      20,
      1
    ],
    "default": 4
  },
  {
    "id": "labelsVisible",
    "type": "toggle",
    "label": "Show labels",
    "teaches": "Whether the learner can name each part of the apparatus — source, collimated beam, foil, and encircling screen — without prompting.",
    "default": true
  },
  {
    "id": "orbitScene",
    "type": "orbit",
    "teaches": "The detector is a full circle surrounding the foil, not a flat plate behind it, which is why deflections at any angle — even backwards toward the source — can be recorded."
  }
]

DEMO STEPS (tween through these using goToStep(); each step drives the controls above):
[
  {
    "title": "The apparatus",
    "narration": "A radium source sealed in a lead block emits alpha particles; a narrow bore collimates them into a single green beam aimed at a thin gold foil sitting at the center of a circular luminescent screen.",
    "state": {
      "labelsVisible": true,
      "foilThickness": 4
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Fire the beam",
    "narration": "Watch the particles stream in. Nearly all punch straight through and flash on the far wall of the screen; a few swerve by a small angle; one in many thousands ricochets wildly, some back toward the source.",
    "state": {
      "labelsVisible": true,
      "foilThickness": 4
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Thicken the foil",
    "narration": "Increase the number of atomic layers. Small-angle deflections become far more common because each particle grazes more atoms — yet the rate of violent backscatter barely moves.",
    "state": {
      "labelsVisible": true,
      "foilThickness": 18
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Thin it back down",
    "narration": "With a single atomic layer, almost everything goes straight through — but the rare large-angle events persist. Something extremely small, dense and charged must sit inside each atom.",
    "state": {
      "labelsVisible": true,
      "foilThickness": 1
    }
  },
  {
    "title": "Self-quiz",
    "narration": "Labels off. Identify the source housing, the collimated beam, the gold foil, and the luminescent screen from geometry alone.",
    "state": {
      "labelsVisible": false,
      "foilThickness": 4
    }
  }
]

CAMERA VIEW (framing only). Call setCameraView({ projection, ...these }) AFTER all geometry is added. Keep the projection MECHANISM from PROJECTION TYPE above (orthographic / sheared-oblique / perspective). For perspective these params set the pose (azimuth/elevation/roll); for the two parallel projections pose is a standard default (see PROJECTION TYPE above), and these params set only roll and framing (heightFraction):
{
  "rollDeg": 0,
